A detailed likelihood analysis is performed on the general two Higgs doublet model in order to assess its ability to explain observed anomalies in rare decays of $B$ mesons, particularly the anomalies associated with $b	o c l \overline{
u}$ and $b	o s\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ transitions. This analysis finds that the general two Higgs doublet model offers a satisfactory fit to available data on $b	o c l \overline{
u}$ transitions and improves upon the minimal two Higgs doublet model in fitting $b	o s\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ transitions. However, it fails to adequately explain observed anomalies when considered separately. Furthermore, within the framework of perturbative theory, a combination of experimental data on $b	o c l \overline{
u}$ and $b	o s\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ transitions indicates that the general two Higgs doublet model is preferred over both the minimal two Higgs doublet model and the standard model of particle physics. This analysis is based on a methodology that accounts for uncertainties in semileptonic decays and employs a matched or mismatched perturbative framework for calculations. Additionally, it has been verified that in the standard model, the observed anomalies in $b	o s\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ transitions are within one standard deviation when considering $\sqrt{c_{
